From ff9c797ab74ca75fda96f41c7a0fc7e9fe51353a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tim <107831991+SyntaxError-PEBKAC@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 15:06:49 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 21/22] ducksteps: fade the logo's edges instead of cutting it
 off square

The logo artwork runs straight off its own frame. On about-logo@2x.png
195 rows of fully opaque pixels sit against the right edge, slicing
through the middle of the silver swoosh, and a smaller cut does the same
on the left. Against a dark backdrop that reads as a sharp vertical seam
rather than as artwork.

Two fades, kept independent:

  * an outline feather along the artwork's real silhouette, so the icon
    melts into the backdrop instead of ending on a crisp vector edge;
  * a wider fade down the right-hand side, following a shallow arc, so
    the swoosh dissolves where it is cut rather than stopping dead.

They are separate because the right frame edge is not a silhouette
boundary - the arc already owns it. Treating it as one as well made the
two fades compound and eat the swoosh: the silver lost its brightness
early and read as a grey smear against the navy, which looked worse than
the hard edge it replaced. The arc's radius matters for the same reason.
It has to stay large, because the swoosh is at its widest and brightest
near the top of the cut, and a tight arc pulls the fade far enough in
there to swallow the band.

The outline falloff comes from a Gaussian-blurred silhouette rather than
a distance transform. A chamfer distance quantises, and its contours
staircase wherever the boundary runs close to horizontal, which showed
up as a visible comb along the top edge of the swoosh.

RGB is untouched throughout; only alpha moves.

Applied to every raster carrying the same cut, so no surface is left as
the odd one out: about-logo.png and @2x (About dialog, New Tab at 64px,
the #fxms-bmb-button toolbar icon, onboarding), about-logo-private.png
and @2x (about:privatebrowsing), and the 512px PNG embedded in
about-logo.svg (profile selector, More from Mozilla, welcome callouts).
Checked at 192px, 64px and 16px on both dark and light surfaces. Costs
about 20KB across the five files.

soften.py is the script that produced them, kept alongside the artwork
because the transform cannot be recovered from the results - if the icon
is ever redrawn, the fade has to be reapplied from this. Running it with
no arguments regenerates all five in place, and it reproduces the assets
in this commit byte for byte from their predecessors. It refuses to
touch a file that already looks softened, because applying it twice
compounds and eats the artwork. It sits outside content/ and is not
listed in jar.mn, so it is not packaged.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
